 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
 *   **Precision:** Floating-point comparisons for boundary checks ($p \in [-2^{d-1}, 2^{d-1})$) must handle edge cases where $p$ is exactly on the boundary [S1, unofficial]. The interval is half-open, so $p = 2^{d-1}$ triggers growth, while $p = -2^{d-1}$ does not [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Bitstream Consistency:** The bitstream used for insertion must be consistent with the coordinate system [S1, unofficial]. If the octree grows, the bitstream interpretation for existing nodes must remain valid relative to the new root [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Allocation:** Creating new nodes during insertion and growth can lead to memory fragmentation [S1, unofficial]. Efficient allocators or pool-based allocation are recommended [S1, unofficial].
